/*! \page developer_backend_own_page Ownership Hierarchy

\ref developer_backend_id_page      (Prev/Up) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_device_page  (Next)    &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

Each executable program created by the PSCF package is designed 
using a hierarchical system of object ownership. All but one of the 
objects used in a PSCF program is a "child" that is owned by an instance 
of some higher-level "parent" class.  In this system, each "child" object 
is either a member variable of its parent, or a dynamically allocated 
object that is accessed through a pointer that is a member variable of 
the parent object. Each parent is responsible for creating and 
destroying its children.

The top of the ownership hierarchy used by the pscf_rpc and pscf_rpg
programs is an instance of a specialization of the System \<D,T\> class 
template. For this and may other templates in the Pscf::Prdc and Pscf::Rp 
namespaces, D is an int template parameter that represents the dimension 
of space, while T is typename parameter that takes a backend identifier 
class as its argument. The top-level object used by the pscf_rpc program
is an instance of a specialization System\<D,T\> with T=Pscf::CPT, while
the top-level object used by pscf_rpc program is an instance of a 
specialization with T=Pscf::CUT.  A value for the integer D is passed 
to the pscf_rpc and pscf_rpg programs as a command line parameter, as 
the parameter of command line option -d.  Thus, for example, if the 
pscf_rpg program is invoked with a command line option "-d 3", as
\code
   pscf_rpg -d 3 -p param -c command
\endcode
then this program constructs an instance of the class 
Rp::System\<3, CUT\>.  Member functions of this System object are then 
invoked to read the parameter and command files, whose names are also 
passed to the program as command line parameters.

In the Rp namespace, the same arguments for the parameters D and 
T are used through objects in the ownership hierarchy: 
An object that is an instance of a class template specialization with 
a specific pair of arguments for D and T may own children that are 
instances of templates specializations with the same two template 
arguments, and may be a child of a parent that is an instance of 
another class template specialization with the same two values for 
these two parameter.

For exmaple, the children of an instance of System\<D,T\> include 
instances of the following class template specializations:
    - Rp::Mixture\<D,T\>
    - Rp::Domain \<D,T\>
    - Rp::CFields \<D,T\>
    - Rp::WFields \<D,T\>

Here and hereafter, we will often use Rp:: to denote the namespace
Pscf::Rp.  

An instance of Rp::Mixture\<D,T\> owns a set of children that each solve
the modified diffusion equation for particular molecular species. Among
these are objects associated with particular block polymer species, which 
are instances of Rp::Polymer\<D,T\>, and other objects associated with 
particular solvent species, which are instances of Rp::Solvent\<D,T\>). 
Each instance of Rp::Polymer\<D,T\> has a set of child objects associated 
with different blocks within a block polymer, which are instances of 
Rp::Block\<D,T\>), as well as objects associated with propagators, which
are instances of Rp::Propagator\<D,T\>. Each propagator is associated 
with traversal of a block in one of two possible directions. 

An instance of Rp::WFields \<D,T\> owns a set of objects that store 
chemical potential fields associated with different monomer types. 
An instance of Rp::CFields\<D,T\> owns a set of objects that store 
monomer concentration fields associated with different monomer types. 

A instance of Rp::Domain\<D,T\> owns an instance of UnitCell\<D,T\>
that contains information about the periodic simulation unit cell, 
as well as other objects that contain information about space group
symmetries.

An instance of System \<D,T\> with a specific choice of values for the 
template parameters D and T thus directly or indirectly owns a large set 
of instances of other class template specializations that use the same 
arguments for these two template parameters. All of these objects are 
thus designed to use the same computational backend, and to treat 
systems with the same spatial dimension D.

The ability to create separate template specializations for models with 
different values of D is used primarily for SCFT calculations, for which 
on might use D=1 to treat a lamellar phase or D=2 to treat a 2D hexagonal 
phase. PS-FTS simulations generally use a value D=3, in order to allow 
the fluctuating fields to depends on three spatial coordinates.

<BR>
\ref developer_backend_page         (Prev/Up) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_device_page  (Next)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;

*/
